import Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.List.Basic
import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Action.Basic
import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Subgroup.Pointwise
import Mathlib.Data.List.Basic
import Mathlib.Data.List.Chain
import Mathlib.Data.Set.Lattice
import Mathlib.Tactic.Group

/-!
# Spellings in the group generated by a windmill and a set of rotations

Dahmani--Guirardel--Osin, *Hyperbolically embedded subgroups and rotating
families in groups acting on hyperbolic spaces*, Mem. Amer. Math. Soc. 245
(2017), no. 1156, §5.3.  This is the word-combinatorial layer of the windmill
induction that proves their Theorem 5.3(b).

At each stage of that induction one has a windmill `W` with its group `G_W`, a
set `C₁` of apices, and one passes to

    `G₂ = ⟨G_W, Rot d : d ∈ C₁⟩`.

Every element of `G₂` is a product

    `a₀ · b₁ a₁ · b₂ a₂ ⋯ b_k a_k`,  `a_j ∈ G_W`,  `b_j ∈ Rot d_j \ {1}`,
    `d_j ∈ C₁`,

and the whole of DGO's argument is run on such a word: the *developed apices*
`P_j = (a₀ b₁ a₁ ⋯ b_{j-1} a_{j-1}) · d_j` are the apices that the word actually
rotates about, and the geometry is applied to the broken path through them.
This module supplies the algebra of those words and **nothing geometric**: no
metric, no hyperbolicity, no separation.  It is stated over a bare
`MulAction G X`, so it is available at every stage of the induction and at every
model, degenerate ones included.

## The encoding, and its dictionary to DGO's word

A `Syll` carries three fields `pre`, `dir`, `rot`, and a `Spelling` is a list of
syllables together with one trailing letter `tail`, evaluating to

    `p₁ b₁ · p₂ b₂ ⋯ p_k b_k · e`.

This regroups DGO's word by putting each `G_W` letter **before** the rotation
that follows it rather than after the one that precedes it:

    `p₁ = a₀`,  `p_{j+1} = a_j` for `1 ≤ j < k`,  `e = a_k`.

The regrouping is not cosmetic, and it is the one design decision in the module.
Every operation the induction performs -- left multiplication by a generator,
merging two adjacent syllables, cyclic permutation -- has to absorb a `G_W`
element at *one end* of what remains.  In DGO's grouping that end is the **last
tail**, and list surgery on the last element of a list needs `dropLast` and
`getLast` and produces a three-clause recursion whose equation lemmas are then
carried through every proof.  In this grouping the same absorption is a rewrite
of the **head** of the list, that is, a two-case match on `[]` and `w :: l`
(`Spelling.lmul` below), and the merge, the reduction and the cyclic step are
all cons-shaped.  Under the dictionary above the two encodings define the same
words, the same notion of reduced, and the same developed apices; the statements
below are recorded in both vocabularies so that a consumer written from DGO's
page can read them.

## What is proved

* `exists_isValid_of_mem_stepGroup` -- **every element of `G₂` has a spelling**,
  by `Subgroup.closure_induction_left` over the generating set
  `G_W ∪ ⋃_{d ∈ C₁} Rot d`.  The left-handed induction principle is what makes
  this cheap: prepending a generator to a spelling is a one-line construction in
  either case (`Spelling.lmul` for a `G_W` generator, `Spelling.cons` for a
  rotation), whereas the two-sided `Subgroup.closure_induction` would need a
  concatenation *and* a reversal.  The generating set is symmetric, so the
  inverse case is the same construction applied to `x⁻¹`.
* `eval_mem_stepGroup` -- the converse: a valid spelling evaluates into `G₂`.
* `exists_reduced` -- **every element has a reduced spelling**, of no greater
  length.  A spelling is `Reduced` when no two adjacent syllables merge:
  `p_j • d_j ≠ d_{j-1}`, which is DGO's `a_{j-1} · d_j ≠ d_{j-1}`.  When it
  fails, `exists_isValid_shorter_of_seam` merges the pair and the length drops
  by one or by two, so a bounded induction on the length terminates.  DGO say
  "take a spelling of minimal length"; the bounded induction is the same
  argument with the minimisation inlined, and it avoids putting a
  `DecidablePred` on the set of achievable lengths.
* `Spelling.isChain_ne_apices` -- **for a reduced spelling the developed apices
  of adjacent syllables are distinct.**  This is the point of the whole
  encoding, and it is an equivalence rather than an implication: writing
  `g_{j-1}` for the prefix `p₁ b₁ ⋯ p_{j-1} b_{j-1}`, the rotation `b_j` fixes
  `d_j`, so

      `P_j = (g_{j-1} p_j) • d_j = (g_{j-1} p_j b_j) • d_j`  and
      `P_{j+1} = (g_{j-1} p_j b_j p_{j+1}) • d_{j+1}`,

  and cancelling the common group element leaves `d_j = p_{j+1} • d_{j+1}`,
  which is exactly the failure of reducedness at that seam.  So reducedness *is*
  the statement that consecutive developed apices differ, and that is the only
  fact about the word the geometry of the induction consumes.

  Distinctness of *all* the apices, not merely of adjacent ones, is a geometric
  statement -- it needs the separation of the family -- and is deliberately not
  attempted here.

## The hypotheses, and why there are only two

`rot_fix` (a rotation fixes its own apex) and `rot_conj` (equivariance,
`a (Rot d) a⁻¹ = Rot (a · d)`) are the two clauses of
`HullSC.IsRotatingFamily` that survive when the metric is dropped, and they are
taken as bare hypotheses rather than as that structure so that this module does
not import the geometry.  Note what is *not* needed: the invariance of `C₁`
under `G_W`.  In DGO's grouping the merge of two syllables replaces a direction
`d_{j-1}` by `a_{j-1} · d_j`, which then has to be checked to lie in `C₁`; in
the grouping used here the merged syllable keeps the direction `d_{j-1}` it
already had, and no direction in any spelling below is ever moved.  The
invariance is a true and available fact, but nothing here spends it.

## Model tests

Both of the campaign's standing models were run against every definition and
every statement in this file.

*The one-point space.*  `X` a point, the action trivial, `C₁` its one direction.
Then `p • d = d` always, so `Seam` is identically false and `Reduced` holds
exactly for the lists of length at most one.  That is not a degeneracy in the
definition but the correct answer: with a trivial action the equivariance
hypothesis says that every `Rot d` is normalised by `G_W`, so `G₂ = Rot d · G_W`
and every element really is `b · a`, with one syllable at most.
`exists_reduced` therefore asserts something true and sharp there, and
`Spelling.isChain_ne_apices` is vacuous, as it must be: all the apices are the
one point, so a spelling with two syllables could not have distinct apices, and
indeed no reduced spelling has two syllables.

*The infinite dihedral model.*  `X = ℝ`, apices `200δ · ℤ`, `Rot c` generated by
the reflection at `c`; take `G_W = Rot 0` and `C₁ = {p}` for one apex `p ≠ 0`,
so that `G₂ = ⟨σ₀, σ_p⟩` is infinite dihedral.  Here `Rot p \ {1} = {σ_p}` and
`G_W = {1, σ₀}`, so a spelling is `p₁ σ_p p₂ σ_p ⋯ σ_p e` with each `p_j` either
`1` or `σ₀`; and `p_j • p = p` exactly when `p_j = 1`.  So `Reduced` says that
every interior `p_j` is `σ₀`, that is, the reduced spellings are exactly the
alternating words `σ₀ σ_p σ₀ σ_p ⋯`, which is the normal form of `ℤ/2 ∗ ℤ/2`.
The developed apices of `(σ_p σ₀)^n` are `p, 3p, 5p, …`, pairwise distinct, and
`Spelling.isChain_ne_apices` gives the adjacent half of that.
-/

namespace GroupApproximation
namespace DGOWindmill

universe u v

/-! ## Syllables and spellings -/

/-- One **syllable** of a windmill spelling: an element of `G_W`, a direction,
and a rotation about that direction, read in that order.

The `G_W` element is carried *before* the rotation rather than after it; see the
dictionary in the module docstring for the translation to
Dahmani--Guirardel--Osin's grouping. -/
structure Syll (G : Type u) (X : Type v) where
  /-- The element of `G_W` read before the rotation. -/
  pre : G
  /-- The direction (apex) that the rotation is about. -/
  dir : X
  /-- The rotation, an element of `Rot dir`. -/
  rot : G

/-- A **spelling**: a list of syllables followed by one trailing element of
`G_W`.  Its value is `p₁ b₁ · p₂ b₂ ⋯ p_k b_k · e`. -/
structure Spelling (G : Type u) (X : Type v) where
  /-- The syllables, in reading order. -/
  sylls : List (Syll G X)
  /-- The element of `G_W` read after the last rotation. -/
  tail : G

section Data

variable {G : Type u} {X : Type v}

@[simp]
theorem Syll.pre_mk (a : G) (d : X) (b : G) : (Syll.mk a d b).pre = a := rfl

@[simp]
theorem Syll.dir_mk (a : G) (d : X) (b : G) : (Syll.mk a d b).dir = d := rfl

@[simp]
theorem Syll.rot_mk (a : G) (d : X) (b : G) : (Syll.mk a d b).rot = b := rfl

@[simp]
theorem Spelling.sylls_mk (l : List (Syll G X)) (e : G) : (Spelling.mk l e).sylls = l := rfl

@[simp]
theorem Spelling.tail_mk (l : List (Syll G X)) (e : G) : (Spelling.mk l e).tail = e := rfl

/-- Prepend a syllable to a spelling. -/
def Spelling.cons (w : Syll G X) (s : Spelling G X) : Spelling G X :=
  ⟨w :: s.sylls, s.tail⟩

/-- Prepend a list of syllables to a spelling. -/
def Spelling.appendList (l : List (Syll G X)) (s : Spelling G X) : Spelling G X :=
  ⟨l ++ s.sylls, s.tail⟩

@[simp]
theorem Spelling.sylls_cons (w : Syll G X) (s : Spelling G X) :
    (Spelling.cons w s).sylls = w :: s.sylls := rfl

@[simp]
theorem Spelling.tail_cons (w : Syll G X) (s : Spelling G X) :
    (Spelling.cons w s).tail = s.tail := rfl

@[simp]
theorem Spelling.sylls_appendList (l : List (Syll G X)) (s : Spelling G X) :
    (Spelling.appendList l s).sylls = l ++ s.sylls := rfl

@[simp]
theorem Spelling.tail_appendList (l : List (Syll G X)) (s : Spelling G X) :
    (Spelling.appendList l s).tail = s.tail := rfl

end Data

/-! ## Evaluation -/

section Eval

variable {G : Type u} [Group G] {X : Type v}

/-- The value of a syllable: the `G_W` letter followed by the rotation. -/
def Syll.eval (w : Syll G X) : G := w.pre * w.rot

/-- The value of a list of syllables. -/
def listEval (l : List (Syll G X)) : G := (l.map Syll.eval).prod

@[simp]
theorem listEval_nil : listEval ([] : List (Syll G X)) = 1 := rfl

@[simp]
theorem listEval_cons (w : Syll G X) (l : List (Syll G X)) :
    listEval (w :: l) = w.pre * w.rot * listEval l := by
  simp [listEval, Syll.eval]

theorem listEval_append (l₁ l₂ : List (Syll G X)) :
    listEval (l₁ ++ l₂) = listEval l₁ * listEval l₂ := by
  simp [listEval, List.map_append, List.prod_append]

/-- The value of a spelling. -/
def Spelling.eval (s : Spelling G X) : G := listEval s.sylls * s.tail

@[simp]
theorem Spelling.eval_mk (l : List (Syll G X)) (e : G) :
    (Spelling.mk l e).eval = listEval l * e := rfl

theorem Spelling.eval_def (s : Spelling G X) : s.eval = listEval s.sylls * s.tail := rfl

/-- The empty spelling, of value `1`. -/
def Spelling.one : Spelling G X := ⟨[], 1⟩

@[simp]
theorem Spelling.eval_one : (Spelling.one : Spelling G X).eval = 1 := by
  simp [Spelling.one]

theorem Spelling.eval_cons (w : Syll G X) (s : Spelling G X) :
    (Spelling.cons w s).eval = w.pre * w.rot * s.eval := by
  simp [Spelling.cons, Spelling.eval, mul_assoc]

theorem Spelling.eval_appendList (l : List (Syll G X)) (s : Spelling G X) :
    (Spelling.appendList l s).eval = listEval l * s.eval := by
  simp [Spelling.appendList, Spelling.eval, listEval_append, mul_assoc]

/-- **Left multiplication by an element of `G_W`.**  The new letter is absorbed
into the first `pre`, or into the trailing letter when there are no syllables.

This is the operation the whole module is arranged around: a two-case match on
the head of the list, with no recursion. -/
def Spelling.lmul (a : G) : Spelling G X → Spelling G X
  | ⟨[], e⟩ => ⟨[], a * e⟩
  | ⟨w :: l, e⟩ => ⟨{ w with pre := a * w.pre } :: l, e⟩

@[simp]
theorem Spelling.lmul_nil (a e : G) :
    Spelling.lmul a (⟨[], e⟩ : Spelling G X) = ⟨[], a * e⟩ := rfl

@[simp]
theorem Spelling.lmul_cons (a : G) (w : Syll G X) (l : List (Syll G X)) (e : G) :
    Spelling.lmul a (⟨w :: l, e⟩ : Spelling G X)
      = ⟨{ w with pre := a * w.pre } :: l, e⟩ := rfl

theorem Spelling.eval_lmul (a : G) (s : Spelling G X) :
    (Spelling.lmul a s).eval = a * s.eval := by
  obtain ⟨l, e⟩ := s
  cases l with
  | nil => simp
  | cons w l => simp [mul_assoc]

@[simp]
theorem Spelling.length_sylls_lmul (a : G) (s : Spelling G X) :
    (Spelling.lmul a s).sylls.length = s.sylls.length := by
  obtain ⟨l, e⟩ := s
  cases l with
  | nil => simp
  | cons w l => simp

end Eval

/-! ## Validity -/

section Valid

variable {G : Type u} [Group G] {X : Type v}
  {Rot : X → Subgroup G} {C₁ : Set X} {GW : Subgroup G}

/-- A syllable is **valid** when its letter lies in `G_W`, its direction in
`C₁`, and its rotation is a nontrivial element of the rotation subgroup at that
direction. -/
structure Syll.IsValid (Rot : X → Subgroup G) (C₁ : Set X) (GW : Subgroup G)
    (w : Syll G X) : Prop where
  /-- The `G_W` letter lies in `G_W`. -/
  pre_mem : w.pre ∈ GW
  /-- The direction is one of the apices of the stage. -/
  dir_mem : w.dir ∈ C₁
  /-- The rotation lies in the rotation subgroup at that direction. -/
  rot_mem : w.rot ∈ Rot w.dir
  /-- The rotation is nontrivial: a trivial one would be no syllable at all. -/
  rot_ne_one : w.rot ≠ 1

/-- A spelling is **valid** when its trailing letter lies in `G_W` and every
syllable is valid. -/
structure Spelling.IsValid (Rot : X → Subgroup G) (C₁ : Set X) (GW : Subgroup G)
    (s : Spelling G X) : Prop where
  /-- The trailing letter lies in `G_W`. -/
  tail_mem : s.tail ∈ GW
  /-- Every syllable is valid. -/
  sylls_valid : ∀ w ∈ s.sylls, w.IsValid Rot C₁ GW

theorem Spelling.isValid_one : (Spelling.one : Spelling G X).IsValid Rot C₁ GW := by
  refine ⟨GW.one_mem, ?_⟩
  intro x hx
  simp [Spelling.one] at hx

theorem Spelling.IsValid.cons {w : Syll G X} {s : Spelling G X}
    (hw : w.IsValid Rot C₁ GW) (hs : s.IsValid Rot C₁ GW) :
    (Spelling.cons w s).IsValid Rot C₁ GW := by
  refine ⟨hs.tail_mem, ?_⟩
  intro x hx
  simp only [Spelling.sylls_cons, List.mem_cons] at hx
  rcases hx with rfl | hx'
  · exact hw
  · exact hs.sylls_valid x hx'

theorem Spelling.IsValid.appendList {l : List (Syll G X)} {s : Spelling G X}
    (hl : ∀ w ∈ l, w.IsValid Rot C₁ GW) (hs : s.IsValid Rot C₁ GW) :
    (Spelling.appendList l s).IsValid Rot C₁ GW := by
  refine ⟨hs.tail_mem, ?_⟩
  intro x hx
  simp only [Spelling.sylls_appendList, List.mem_append] at hx
  rcases hx with hx' | hx'
  · exact hl x hx'
  · exact hs.sylls_valid x hx'

theorem Spelling.IsValid.lmul {a : G} (ha : a ∈ GW) {s : Spelling G X}
    (hs : s.IsValid Rot C₁ GW) : (Spelling.lmul a s).IsValid Rot C₁ GW := by
  obtain ⟨l, e⟩ := s
  cases l with
  | nil =>
      refine ⟨GW.mul_mem ha hs.tail_mem, ?_⟩
      intro x hx
      simp at hx
  | cons w l =>
      refine ⟨hs.tail_mem, ?_⟩
      intro x hx
      simp only [Spelling.lmul_cons, List.mem_cons] at hx
      have hw : w.IsValid Rot C₁ GW := hs.sylls_valid w (by simp)
      rcases hx with rfl | hx'
      · exact ⟨GW.mul_mem ha hw.pre_mem, hw.dir_mem, hw.rot_mem, hw.rot_ne_one⟩
      · exact hs.sylls_valid x (by simp [hx'])

end Valid

/-! ## The group of the next windmill, and existence of spellings -/

section Existence

variable {G : Type u} [Group G] {X : Type v}
  {Rot : X → Subgroup G} {C₁ : Set X} {GW : Subgroup G}

/-- The generating set of `G₂`: the windmill group together with every rotation
about every apex of the stage. -/
def genSet (Rot : X → Subgroup G) (C₁ : Set X) (GW : Subgroup G) : Set G :=
  {g : G | g ∈ GW ∨ ∃ d ∈ C₁, g ∈ Rot d}

theorem mem_genSet_iff {x : G} :
    x ∈ genSet Rot C₁ GW ↔ x ∈ GW ∨ ∃ d ∈ C₁, x ∈ Rot d := Iff.rfl

theorem mem_genSet_of_mem_gw {x : G} (hx : x ∈ GW) : x ∈ genSet Rot C₁ GW :=
  Or.inl hx

theorem mem_genSet_of_mem_rot {d : X} (hd : d ∈ C₁) {x : G} (hx : x ∈ Rot d) :
    x ∈ genSet Rot C₁ GW :=
  Or.inr ⟨d, hd, hx⟩

/-- The generating set is symmetric, which is what collapses the two generator
cases of `Subgroup.closure_induction_left` into one. -/
theorem inv_mem_genSet {x : G} (hx : x ∈ genSet Rot C₁ GW) :
    x⁻¹ ∈ genSet Rot C₁ GW := by
  rcases mem_genSet_iff.mp hx with h | ⟨d, hd, h⟩
  · exact Or.inl (GW.inv_mem h)
  · exact Or.inr ⟨d, hd, (Rot d).inv_mem h⟩

/-- **`G₂`**: the group generated by the windmill group and the rotations about
the apices of the stage. -/
def stepGroup (Rot : X → Subgroup G) (C₁ : Set X) (GW : Subgroup G) : Subgroup G :=
  Subgroup.closure (genSet Rot C₁ GW)

/-- The generating set in union form.  `genSet` is written as a `setOf` so that
`mem_genSet_iff` is `Iff.rfl` and the induction below never has to unfold a
lattice operation; this lemma is the bridge to the union a consumer will want to
match against `Subgroup.closure` of an explicit union. -/
theorem genSet_eq_union :
    genSet Rot C₁ GW = (GW : Set G) ∪ ⋃ d ∈ C₁, (Rot d : Set G) := by
  ext x
  simp [genSet]

/-- **`G₂` as the closure of an explicit union**, the form the windmill growth
step is stated against. -/
theorem stepGroup_eq_closure_union :
    stepGroup Rot C₁ GW
      = Subgroup.closure ((GW : Set G) ∪ ⋃ d ∈ C₁, (Rot d : Set G)) :=
  congrArg Subgroup.closure genSet_eq_union

theorem gw_le_stepGroup : GW ≤ stepGroup Rot C₁ GW := fun _ hx =>
  Subgroup.subset_closure (mem_genSet_of_mem_gw hx)

theorem rot_le_stepGroup {d : X} (hd : d ∈ C₁) : Rot d ≤ stepGroup Rot C₁ GW :=
  fun _ hx => Subgroup.subset_closure (mem_genSet_of_mem_rot hd hx)

theorem listEval_mem_stepGroup {l : List (Syll G X)}
    (hl : ∀ w ∈ l, w.IsValid Rot C₁ GW) : listEval l ∈ stepGroup Rot C₁ GW := by
  induction l with
  | nil => exact (stepGroup Rot C₁ GW).one_mem
  | cons w l ih =>
      have hw : w.IsValid Rot C₁ GW := hl w (by simp)
      have hrest : listEval l ∈ stepGroup Rot C₁ GW :=
        ih fun x hx => hl x (by simp [hx])
      rw [listEval_cons]
      exact (stepGroup Rot C₁ GW).mul_mem
        ((stepGroup Rot C₁ GW).mul_mem (gw_le_stepGroup hw.pre_mem)
          (rot_le_stepGroup hw.dir_mem hw.rot_mem)) hrest

/-- A valid spelling evaluates into `G₂`. -/
theorem eval_mem_stepGroup {s : Spelling G X} (hs : s.IsValid Rot C₁ GW) :
    s.eval ∈ stepGroup Rot C₁ GW :=
  (stepGroup Rot C₁ GW).mul_mem (listEval_mem_stepGroup hs.sylls_valid)
    (gw_le_stepGroup hs.tail_mem)

/-- **Prepending a generator to a spelling.**  A `G_W` generator is absorbed by
`Spelling.lmul`; a rotation becomes a new leading syllable, unless it is
trivial, in which case there is nothing to do. -/
theorem exists_isValid_mul {x : G} (hx : x ∈ genSet Rot C₁ GW) {y : G}
    {s : Spelling G X} (hs : s.IsValid Rot C₁ GW) (hse : s.eval = y) :
    ∃ t : Spelling G X, t.IsValid Rot C₁ GW ∧ t.eval = x * y := by
  rcases mem_genSet_iff.mp hx with hxW | ⟨d, hd, hxd⟩
  · exact ⟨Spelling.lmul x s, hs.lmul hxW, by rw [Spelling.eval_lmul, hse]⟩
  · by_cases hx1 : x = 1
    · exact ⟨s, hs, by rw [hse, hx1, one_mul]⟩
    · refine ⟨Spelling.cons ⟨1, d, x⟩ s, ?_, ?_⟩
      · exact Spelling.IsValid.cons ⟨GW.one_mem, hd, hxd, hx1⟩ hs
      · rw [Spelling.eval_cons, hse]
        simp

/-- **Every element of `G₂` has a spelling.**

The proof is `Subgroup.closure_induction_left`, which builds the word one
generator at a time from the left; the generating set is symmetric, so the
inverse case is the same construction applied to `x⁻¹`. -/
theorem exists_isValid_of_mem_stepGroup {g : G} (hg : g ∈ stepGroup Rot C₁ GW) :
    ∃ s : Spelling G X, s.IsValid Rot C₁ GW ∧ s.eval = g := by
  have hg' : g ∈ Subgroup.closure (genSet Rot C₁ GW) := hg
  -- `hg` mentions `g`, so leaving it in context makes `induction` revert it into
  -- the motive and hand back an induction hypothesis that is an implication.
  clear hg
  induction hg' using Subgroup.closure_induction_left with
  | one => exact ⟨Spelling.one, Spelling.isValid_one, Spelling.eval_one⟩
  | mul_left x hx y _ ih =>
      obtain ⟨s, hs, hse⟩ := ih
      exact exists_isValid_mul hx hs hse
  | inv_mul_cancel x hx y _ ih =>
      obtain ⟨s, hs, hse⟩ := ih
      exact exists_isValid_mul (inv_mem_genSet hx) hs hse

end Existence

/-! ## Reduced spellings -/

section Reduced

variable {G : Type u} [Group G] {X : Type v} [MulAction G X]
  {Rot : X → Subgroup G} {C₁ : Set X} {GW : Subgroup G}

/-- **The seam relation.**  `Seam u v` says that the syllables `u`, `v` do not
merge: the `G_W` letter standing between the two rotations does not carry the
direction of `v` onto the direction of `u`.

In Dahmani--Guirardel--Osin's grouping, with `u` the `(j-1)`-st syllable and `v`
the `j`-th, this reads `a_{j-1} · d_j ≠ d_{j-1}`. -/
def Seam (u v : Syll G X) : Prop := v.pre • v.dir ≠ u.dir

theorem seam_iff {u v : Syll G X} : Seam u v ↔ v.pre • v.dir ≠ u.dir := Iff.rfl

/-- A list of syllables is **reduced** when no adjacent pair merges. -/
def ReducedList (l : List (Syll G X)) : Prop := List.IsChain Seam l

/-- A spelling is **reduced** when its list of syllables is. -/
def Spelling.Reduced (s : Spelling G X) : Prop := ReducedList s.sylls

theorem reducedList_nil : ReducedList ([] : List (Syll G X)) := List.isChain_nil

theorem reducedList_singleton (w : Syll G X) : ReducedList [w] :=
  List.isChain_singleton w

/-- The failure of reducedness, in the shape the merge consumes: a decomposition
of the list at an adjacent pair that does merge. -/
theorem exists_seam_decomp {l : List (Syll G X)} (h : ¬ ReducedList l) :
    ∃ (u v : Syll G X) (l₁ l₂ : List (Syll G X)),
      l = l₁ ++ u :: v :: l₂ ∧ v.pre • v.dir = u.dir := by
  by_contra hc
  refine h (List.isChain_iff_forall_rel_of_append_cons_cons.2 ?_)
  intro a b l₁ l₂ he heq
  exact hc ⟨a, b, l₁, l₂, he, heq⟩

/-- **Merging an adjacent pair that meets.**

If the `G_W` letter `v.pre` carries `v.dir` onto `u.dir`, then
`v.pre (Rot v.dir) v.pre⁻¹ = Rot u.dir`, so the two rotations of the pair can be
brought together into the single rotation

    `m = u.rot · (v.pre · v.rot · v.pre⁻¹) ∈ Rot u.dir`

at the cost of pushing `v.pre` to the right, where `Spelling.lmul` absorbs it.
If `m ≠ 1` the pair becomes one syllable and the length drops by one; if `m = 1`
the pair disappears and the length drops by two.  Either way the value is
unchanged, which is what the reduction below needs. -/
theorem exists_isValid_shorter_of_seam
    (hconj : ∀ (a : G) (d : X), d ∈ C₁ → ∀ b ∈ Rot d, a * b * a⁻¹ ∈ Rot (a • d))
    {s : Spelling G X} (hs : s.IsValid Rot C₁ GW)
    {u v : Syll G X} {l₁ l₂ : List (Syll G X)}
    (hl : s.sylls = l₁ ++ u :: v :: l₂) (hseam : v.pre • v.dir = u.dir) :
    ∃ t : Spelling G X, t.IsValid Rot C₁ GW ∧ t.eval = s.eval ∧
      t.sylls.length < s.sylls.length := by
  have hu : u.IsValid Rot C₁ GW := hs.sylls_valid u (by rw [hl]; simp)
  have hv : v.IsValid Rot C₁ GW := hs.sylls_valid v (by rw [hl]; simp)
  have hl₁ : ∀ x ∈ l₁, x.IsValid Rot C₁ GW := fun x hx =>
    hs.sylls_valid x (by rw [hl]; simp [hx])
  have hl₂ : ∀ x ∈ l₂, x.IsValid Rot C₁ GW := fun x hx =>
    hs.sylls_valid x (by rw [hl]; simp [hx])
  have hrvalid : (⟨l₂, s.tail⟩ : Spelling G X).IsValid Rot C₁ GW :=
    ⟨hs.tail_mem, hl₂⟩
  have hlen : s.sylls.length = l₁.length + l₂.length + 2 := by
    rw [hl]
    simp only [List.length_append, List.length_cons]
    omega
  have hs_eval : s.eval
      = listEval l₁ * (u.pre * u.rot * (v.pre * v.rot * (listEval l₂ * s.tail))) := by
    rw [Spelling.eval_def, hl, listEval_append, listEval_cons, listEval_cons]
    group
  have hconj_v : v.pre * v.rot * v.pre⁻¹ ∈ Rot u.dir := by
    have h := hconj v.pre v.dir hv.dir_mem v.rot hv.rot_mem
    rwa [hseam] at h
  by_cases hm : u.rot * (v.pre * v.rot * v.pre⁻¹) = 1
  · -- The pair cancels: two syllables disappear.
    have hkey : u.rot * (v.pre * v.rot) = v.pre := by
      have h := congrArg (fun z : G => z * v.pre) hm
      simpa [mul_assoc] using h
    have hid : u.pre * v.pre = u.pre * u.rot * (v.pre * v.rot) := by
      rw [mul_assoc, hkey]
    refine ⟨Spelling.appendList l₁ (Spelling.lmul (u.pre * v.pre) ⟨l₂, s.tail⟩),
      ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
    · exact Spelling.IsValid.appendList hl₁
        (hrvalid.lmul (GW.mul_mem hu.pre_mem hv.pre_mem))
    · rw [Spelling.eval_appendList, Spelling.eval_lmul, hs_eval]
      simp only [Spelling.eval_mk]
      rw [hid]
      group
    · simp only [Spelling.sylls_appendList, Spelling.length_sylls_lmul,
        List.length_append, hlen]
      omega
  · -- The pair merges into a single syllable.
    refine ⟨Spelling.appendList l₁
      (Spelling.cons ⟨u.pre, u.dir, u.rot * (v.pre * v.rot * v.pre⁻¹)⟩
        (Spelling.lmul v.pre ⟨l₂, s.tail⟩)), ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
    · refine Spelling.IsValid.appendList hl₁ (Spelling.IsValid.cons ?_ ?_)
      · exact ⟨hu.pre_mem, hu.dir_mem, (Rot u.dir).mul_mem hu.rot_mem hconj_v, hm⟩
      · exact hrvalid.lmul hv.pre_mem
    · rw [Spelling.eval_appendList, Spelling.eval_cons, Spelling.eval_lmul, hs_eval]
      simp only [Spelling.eval_mk]
      group
    · simp only [Spelling.sylls_appendList, Spelling.sylls_cons,
        Spelling.length_sylls_lmul, List.length_append, List.length_cons, hlen]
      omega

/-- **Every element of `G₂` has a reduced spelling**, of no greater length than
any spelling it already has.

Dahmani--Guirardel--Osin take a spelling of minimal length; the bounded
induction below is the same argument with the minimisation inlined. -/
theorem exists_reduced_of_isValid
    (hconj : ∀ (a : G) (d : X), d ∈ C₁ → ∀ b ∈ Rot d, a * b * a⁻¹ ∈ Rot (a • d))
    {s : Spelling G X} (hs : s.IsValid Rot C₁ GW) :
    ∃ t : Spelling G X, t.IsValid Rot C₁ GW ∧ t.eval = s.eval ∧ t.Reduced ∧
      t.sylls.length ≤ s.sylls.length := by
  suffices H : ∀ n : ℕ, ∀ s : Spelling G X, s.IsValid Rot C₁ GW →
      s.sylls.length ≤ n → ∃ t : Spelling G X, t.IsValid Rot C₁ GW ∧
        t.eval = s.eval ∧ t.Reduced ∧ t.sylls.length ≤ s.sylls.length by
    exact H s.sylls.length s hs le_rfl
  intro n
  induction n with
  | zero =>
      intro s hs hlen
      refine ⟨s, hs, rfl, ?_, le_rfl⟩
      have hnil : s.sylls = [] := List.length_eq_zero_iff.mp (Nat.le_zero.mp hlen)
      show ReducedList s.sylls
      rw [hnil]
      exact reducedList_nil
  | succ n ih =>
      intro s hs hlen
      by_cases hred : s.Reduced
      · exact ⟨s, hs, rfl, hred, le_rfl⟩
      · have hred' : ¬ ReducedList s.sylls := hred
        obtain ⟨u, v, l₁, l₂, heq, hmerge⟩ := exists_seam_decomp hred'
        obtain ⟨t, ht, hte, htl⟩ :=
          exists_isValid_shorter_of_seam hconj hs heq hmerge
        obtain ⟨w, hw, hwe, hwr, hwl⟩ := ih t ht (by omega)
        exact ⟨w, hw, hwe.trans hte, hwr, by omega⟩

/-- **Every element of `G₂` has a reduced spelling.** -/
theorem exists_reduced
    (hconj : ∀ (a : G) (d : X), d ∈ C₁ → ∀ b ∈ Rot d, a * b * a⁻¹ ∈ Rot (a • d))
    {g : G} (hg : g ∈ stepGroup Rot C₁ GW) :
    ∃ t : Spelling G X, t.IsValid Rot C₁ GW ∧ t.eval = g ∧ t.Reduced := by
  obtain ⟨s, hs, hse⟩ := exists_isValid_of_mem_stepGroup hg
  obtain ⟨t, ht, hte, htr, _⟩ := exists_reduced_of_isValid hconj hs
  exact ⟨t, ht, hte.trans hse, htr⟩

end Reduced

/-! ## Developed apices -/

section Apices

variable {G : Type u} [Group G] {X : Type v} [MulAction G X]
  {Rot : X → Subgroup G} {C₁ : Set X} {GW : Subgroup G}

/-- The developed apices of a list of syllables read after a prefix `g`.

Both arguments are taken after the colon so that the recursion may vary the
group element; the argument of the recursive call is the prefix extended by the
syllable just read.  This is the shape of `List.foldl`. -/
def apicesAux : G → List (Syll G X) → List X
  | _, [] => []
  | g, w :: l => (g * w.pre) • w.dir :: apicesAux (g * w.pre * w.rot) l

@[simp]
theorem apicesAux_nil (g : G) : apicesAux g ([] : List (Syll G X)) = [] := by
  simp [apicesAux]

@[simp]
theorem apicesAux_cons (g : G) (w : Syll G X) (l : List (Syll G X)) :
    apicesAux g (w :: l)
      = (g * w.pre) • w.dir :: apicesAux (g * w.pre * w.rot) l := by
  simp [apicesAux]

@[simp]
theorem length_apicesAux (g : G) (l : List (Syll G X)) :
    (apicesAux g l).length = l.length := by
  induction l generalizing g with
  | nil => simp
  | cons w l ih => simp [ih]

/-- **The developed apices of a spelling**: the `j`-th entry is
`(p₁ b₁ ⋯ p_{j-1} b_{j-1} p_j) · d_j`, the apex that the `j`-th rotation of the
word actually rotates about.  In Dahmani--Guirardel--Osin's grouping this is
`P_j = (a₀ b₁ a₁ ⋯ b_{j-1} a_{j-1}) · d_j`. -/
def Spelling.apices (s : Spelling G X) : List X := apicesAux 1 s.sylls

theorem Spelling.apices_def (s : Spelling G X) : s.apices = apicesAux 1 s.sylls := rfl

@[simp]
theorem Spelling.length_apices (s : Spelling G X) :
    s.apices.length = s.sylls.length :=
  length_apicesAux 1 s.sylls

/-- **Equivariance of the developed apices.**  Reading the word after a further
prefix `h` translates every apex by `h`. -/
theorem apicesAux_mul (h g : G) (l : List (Syll G X)) :
    apicesAux (h * g) l = (apicesAux g l).map (fun x => h • x) := by
  induction l generalizing g with
  | nil => simp
  | cons w l ih =>
      have h1 : (h * g * w.pre) • w.dir = h • ((g * w.pre) • w.dir) := by
        rw [← mul_smul, mul_assoc]
      have h2 : h * g * w.pre * w.rot = h * (g * w.pre * w.rot) := by group
      rw [apicesAux_cons, apicesAux_cons, h1, h2, ih]
      simp

theorem Spelling.apicesAux_eq_map (g : G) (s : Spelling G X) :
    apicesAux g s.sylls = s.apices.map (fun x => g • x) := by
  rw [Spelling.apices_def, ← apicesAux_mul, mul_one]

/-- **Reducedness is the distinctness of adjacent developed apices.**

The rotation `w.rot` fixes `w.dir`, so the apex of a syllable is unchanged if
the prefix is extended past that syllable's own rotation; the two adjacent
apices are then the images of `w.dir` and of `v.pre • v.dir` under one and the
same group element, and they agree exactly when the seam merges. -/
theorem isChain_ne_apicesAux
    (hfix : ∀ d ∈ C₁, ∀ b ∈ Rot d, b • d = d) :
    ∀ l : List (Syll G X), (∀ w ∈ l, w.IsValid Rot C₁ GW) → ReducedList l →
      ∀ g : G, List.IsChain (· ≠ ·) (apicesAux g l) := by
  intro l
  induction l with
  | nil =>
      intro _ _ g
      rw [apicesAux_nil]
      exact List.isChain_nil
  | cons w l ih =>
      intro hvalid hred g
      have hw : w.IsValid Rot C₁ GW := hvalid w (by simp)
      have hfixw : w.rot • w.dir = w.dir := hfix w.dir hw.dir_mem w.rot hw.rot_mem
      cases l with
      | nil =>
          rw [apicesAux_cons, apicesAux_nil]
          exact List.isChain_singleton _
      | cons t l' =>
          have hred' : List.IsChain Seam (w :: t :: l') := hred
          obtain ⟨hseam, htail⟩ := List.isChain_cons_cons.mp hred'
          have hseam' : t.pre • t.dir ≠ w.dir := hseam
          rw [apicesAux_cons, apicesAux_cons]
          refine List.isChain_cons_cons.mpr ⟨?_, ?_⟩
          · intro hEq
            have h1 : (g * w.pre) • w.dir = (g * w.pre * w.rot) • w.dir := by
              rw [mul_smul (g * w.pre) w.rot, hfixw]
            have h2 : (g * w.pre * w.rot * t.pre) • t.dir
                = (g * w.pre * w.rot) • (t.pre • t.dir) := mul_smul _ _ _
            have h3 : w.dir = t.pre • t.dir :=
              smul_left_cancel (g * w.pre * w.rot) (by rw [← h1, ← h2]; exact hEq)
            exact hseam' h3.symm
          · have hIH := ih (fun x hx => hvalid x (by simp [hx])) htail
              (g * w.pre * w.rot)
            rwa [apicesAux_cons] at hIH

/-- **For a reduced spelling, adjacent developed apices are distinct.** -/
theorem Spelling.isChain_ne_apices
    (hfix : ∀ d ∈ C₁, ∀ b ∈ Rot d, b • d = d)
    {s : Spelling G X} (hs : s.IsValid Rot C₁ GW) (hred : s.Reduced) :
    List.IsChain (· ≠ ·) s.apices :=
  isChain_ne_apicesAux hfix s.sylls hs.sylls_valid hred 1

end Apices

